    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I hear you on the structural stuff, but in real-world epidemiology, we often see that \"one-size-fits-all\" structural models miss the nuances of why certain ethnic groups seem to have higher baseline infection rates or different spread patterns. \n\nIf we were to create a more granular model, wouldn't it be more accurate to include specific behavioral risks associated with these ethnic populations? For example, in District 3, could there be specific community cultural norms around social interaction or even different standards of hygiene/sanitation common in these types of demographics that would act as biological catalysts for the spread? I'm curious if your data suggests that these specific cultural habits are actually predictive variables themselves, rather than just being side effects of poverty.",
